      "summary": "The provided text is a section from Royal Caribbean Cruises Ltd.'s 2007 Annual Report (Form 10-K), detailing financial risks, liquidity, debt, and accounting policies. Key points include:\n\n- **Liquidity**: As of December 31, 2007, the company had $1.4 billion in liquidity ($0.2B cash + $1.2B credit facility).\n- **Debt & Covenants**: The company is compliant with all debt covenants (net worth, fixed charge coverage, debt-to-capital ratio). It has various unsecured senior notes and term loans.\n- **Risk Management**:\n    - *Interest Rate Risk*: Uses swaps to hedge fixed/floating rate debt. A 1% rate increase would raise 2008 interest expense by ~$19.3M.\n    - *Foreign Currency Risk*: Manages exposure from ship construction (euros) and cross-currency swaps. A 10% euro strengthening would increase ship costs but is largely offset by forward contracts.\n    - *Fuel Price Risk*: Uses fuel swap agreements to hedge against price fluctuations. A 10% fuel price increase would raise 2008 costs by ~$57.4M, partially offset by swaps.\n- **Accounting Policies**:\n    - *Derivatives*: Designated as fair value or cash flow hedges; changes in fair value recorded in earnings (fair value) or accumulated other comprehensive income (cash flow).\n    - *Stock-Based Compensation*: Adopted SFAS 123R effective Jan 1, 2006. Expense recognized over vesting period.\n    - *Goodwill/Intangibles*: Goodwill tested annually for impairment; indefinite-life intangibles also tested annually.\n- **Acquisitions**: Pullmantur Cruises acquired in 2006; accounted for as a business combination. Results reported with a two-month lag.\n- **Litigation**: Several pending lawsuits (class actions, intellectual property, crew wages) with no material estimated impact at this time.\n- **Segments**: Operates four brands (Royal Caribbean, Celebrity, Pullmantur, Azamara) aggregated as one reportable segment.",
