      "sourceUrl": "https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/753308/000075330806000110/",
      "summary": "The document provides a detailed financial report for Florida Power & Light (FPL) and its subsidiaries, including FPL Energy and FPL Group Capital, covering the nine months ended September 30, 2006, compared to the same period in 2005. Key highlights include:\n\n1. **Revenue Adjustments**: Under a 2005 rate agreement, gross receipts taxes were removed from base rates and collected separately, reducing retail base revenues by approximately $17 million but increasing other cost recovery revenues.\n\n2. **Usage and Revenue Changes**: Customer usage decreased by 1.0% due to warmer weather and price elasticity, leading to a $34 million reduction in retail base revenues. However, an increase in the number of customer accounts (2.0%) added $55 million to revenues. The net effect of tax removal and usage changes resulted in a $42 million decrease in retail base revenues.\n\n3. **Fuel and Cost Recovery**: Fuel and energy charges increased significantly due to higher fuel prices, with FPL recovering previously underrecovered costs from 2005. The sale of the retail gas business reduced fuel expenses by approximately $15 million (three months) and $42 million (nine months).\n\n4. **Storm Restoration Costs**: FPL began recovering 2004 storm restoration costs in February 2005. In May 2006, the FPSC approved bonds to recover unrecovered costs and establish a storm reserve.\n\n5. **Nuclear Operations**: St. Lucie Unit No. 2 completed its refueling outage with no repairs needed for the reactor vessel head. FPL plans to replace the reactor vessel head and steam generators during the fall 2007 outage. Costs are being amortized over five years.\n\n6. **Capital Expenditures**: Significant investments were made in transmission, distribution, and new generating units (e.g., Martin and Manatee sites). The Storm Secure Plan costs are included in projected capital expenditures.\n\n7. **FPL Energy Performance**: FPL Energy's net income increased significantly due to new investments, improved market conditions, and gains from unrealized mark-to-market non-qualifying hedge activities. Operating revenues also increased, driven by favorable market conditions and project additions.\n\n8. **Debt and Liquidity**: FPL Group and FPL maintained strong liquidity positions with available net liquidity of approximately $4.3 billion and $1.9 billion, respectively. Debt levels were managed through a mix of fixed and variable rate debt, with interest rate swaps used to mitigate exposure.\n\n9. **Credit Ratings**: As of November 2, 2006, FPL Group held an A2 (Moody's), A (S&P), and A (Fitch) corporate credit rating, while FPL held an A1 (Moody's), A/A-1 (S&P), and A (Fitch).\n\n10. **Risk Management**: FPL employs value-at-risk (VaR) models to measure market risk and has established policies to manage credit risk through counterparty limits, collateral, and master netting agreements.\n\nOverall, the report reflects a period of strategic growth, investment in infrastructure, and careful management of financial risks amidst changing market conditions and regulatory requirements.",
