      "summary": "The document provides a detailed financial analysis for the three months ended March 31, 2010, covering FPL Group (Florida Power & Light) and its subsidiary NextEra Energy Resources. Key highlights include:\n\n- **Expenses**: O&M expenses increased by $33 million due to higher nuclear/fossil generation, distribution, and insurance costs, partly offset by lower employee benefit and customer service costs. Depreciation and amortization decreased by $22 million, primarily due to lower pass-through costs and the amortization of a depreciation reserve surplus.\n- **Revenues**: Operating revenues increased by $158 million, driven mainly by gains from unrealized mark-to-market non-qualifying hedge activity ($152 million).\n- **NextEra Energy Resources Performance**: Net income for NextEra Energy Resources increased by $139 million (from $228 million in 2009 to $367 million in 2010). Key drivers included new investments, wholesale marketing gains, and unrealized mark-to-market gains.\n- **Liquidity**: FPL Group's total net available liquidity was approximately $4.1 billion at March 31, 2010, with FPL's portion being around $2.0 billion. The company maintained access to credit facilities despite recent credit rating downgrades by Moody's, S&P, and Fitch.\n- **Credit Ratings**: As of April 30, 2010, FPL Group held ratings of Baa1 (Moody's), A- (S&P), and A- (Fitch). FPL held A2 (Moody's), A- (S&P), and A (Fitch).\n- **Market Risk**: The company uses derivative instruments to manage commodity price risk, with a Value-at-Risk (VaR) model employed for trading portfolios. VaR figures were $5 million for NextEra Energy Resources and $68 million for FPL Group as of March 31, 2010.\n- **Interest Rate Risk**: FPL Group manages interest rate exposure through fixed and variable rate debt, interest rate swaps, and monitoring market conditions. A hypothetical 10% decrease in interest rates would increase the net fair value of liabilities by approximately $882 million.\n- **Equity Price Risk**: Marketable equity securities in nuclear decommissioning funds totaled approximately $1,776 million at March 31, 2010. A 10% decrease in stock prices would reduce fair value by $164 million.\n- **Credit Risk**: Counterparty credit risk is managed through established policies, including credit limits, collateral requirements, and master netting agreements. Approximately 98% of FPL Group's counterparty exposure is with investment-grade companies.",
