      "summary": "The provided text is a detailed excerpt from the 2013 Annual Report (Form 10-K) of Eli Lilly and Company. It covers several key areas:\n\n1. **Tax Matters:** The company discusses the resolution of certain tax examinations, noting an immaterial benefit to consolidated results. They made cash payments of approximately $135 million related to tax years 2008-2009. Examinations for 2010-2012 are ongoing and expected to extend beyond the next 12 months. The company recognizes accrued interest and penalties in income tax expense, with specific amounts disclosed for 2011, 2012, and 2013.\n\n2. **Retirement Benefits (Note 15):** This section provides extensive details on defined benefit pension plans and retiree health benefit plans as of December 31, 2013 and 2012. It includes:\n    *   Changes in benefit obligations and plan assets.\n    *   Funded status and amounts recognized in the consolidated balance sheet.\n    *   Weighted-average assumptions (discount rates, expected return on assets, healthcare cost trends).\n    *   Expected future benefit payments through 2023.\n    *   Sensitivity analysis regarding healthcare-cost trend rates.\n    *   Components of net periodic benefit cost.\n    *   Activity in Level 3 fair value measurements for plan investments.\n    *   Information on defined contribution savings plans and other postemployment benefits.\n\n3. **Contingencies (Note 16):** The company outlines various legal actions and investigations, including:\n    *   **Alimta Patent Litigation:** Ongoing U.S. and international patent disputes with generic manufacturers regarding the Alimta drug.\n    *   **Byetta Product Liability Litigation:** Approximately 275 lawsuits involving allegations of cancer caused by Byetta.\n    *   **Prozac® Product Liability Litigation:** Approximately 10 U.S. lawsuits alleging birth defects.\n    *   **Brazil–Employee Litigation:** Lawsuits related to heavy metal exposure at a former Brazilian facility.\n    *   The company states it is self-insured for product liability losses.\n\n4. **Other Comprehensive Income (Loss) (Note 17):** A table summarizes the activity related to each component of other comprehensive income (loss), including foreign currency translation gains/losses, unrealized net gains/losses on securities, defined benefit pension and retiree health benefit plans, and cash flow hedges. It also details reclassifications out of accumulated other comprehensive loss.\n\n5. **Other–Net, (Income) Expense (Note 18):** This note breaks down items like income related to the termination of the exenatide collaboration with Amylin, interest expense/income, and other miscellaneous items.\n\n6. **Segment Information (Note 19):** The company operates in two segments: Human Pharmaceutical Products and Animal Health. It provides revenue and profit data for each segment broken down by therapeutic area (for pharma) and product type (for animal health). Geographic information on revenue and long-lived assets is also provided.\n\n7. **Selected Quarterly Data (Note 20):** Unaudited quarterly data for 2013 and 2012, including revenue, costs, expenses, income, net income, earnings per share, dividends, and stock prices.\n\n8. **Management's Reports:** Includes reports on the responsibility for financial statements, internal controls over financial reporting (with an unqualified opinion from Ernst & Young LLP), and the company's code of conduct.\n\n9. **Independent Registered Public Accounting Firm Reports:** Unqualified opinions on both the consolidated financial statements and internal control over financial reporting as of December 31, 2013.",
